A good Stream pipeline usually looks like:
Source
   ↓
filter()
   ↓
map()
   ↓
sorted() (if needed)
   ↓
collect()/toList()/findFirst()/count()
This is the pattern you'll use in most interview questions.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Remember Forever
Use comparingInt() when comparing primitive int values.

Examples:
Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getSalary)
Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)
Comparator.comparingInt(Product::getPrice)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Whenever your key is the object itself, prefer Function.identity() over x -> x.
Function.identity() literally means:

"Return the object as it is."

----------------------------------------------------------------------
🧠 Interview Tip

Remember this syntax:

groupingBy(keyExtractor)

is internally equivalent to:

groupingBy(
    keyExtractor,
    Collectors.toList()
)

So whenever you see groupingBy, think:

"What collector do I want inside each group?"

Possible downstream collectors:

toList()
counting()
mapping()
averagingInt()
summingInt()
summarizingInt()
maxBy()
minBy()
collectingAndThen()

This mindset will make advanced collector questions much easier.

⭐ Remember Forever

groupingBy has a default downstream collector of toList().


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 This is an important lesson

Ask yourself:

"What should be the value type of my Map?"

If the value should be:

List<T> → toList()
Set<T> → toSet()
String → joining()
Long → counting()
Double → averagingInt()

The downstream collector determines the value type.

⭐ Remember Forever

mapping() only transforms the elements. The final value type depends on the downstream collector.

mapping(
    transform,
    downstreamCollector
)

For example:

mapping(Employee::getName, toList())

↓

Map<Dept, List<String>>


